<title>USA: Rains aid Minnesota crops, more showers needed.</title>
<headline>Rains aid Minnesota crops, more showers needed.</headline>
<dateline>ST PAUL, Minn. 1997-06-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>Minnesota crops received needed rains in the week to Sunday but more showers are needed, the state's Agricultural Statistics Service said.</p>
<p>&quot;Much of Minnesota received some rain over the last week, helping the crops for the short term. However, much of the state was still several inches below normal in precipitation,&quot; the report said.</p>
<p>Topsoil moisture was rated 65 percent short or very short as of Friday, down from 70 percent a week earlier.</p>
<p>Average corn height was 14 inches, an inch behind normal. Corn was rated 49 percent good or excellent.</p>
<p>Soybean height was five inches on average, an inch shorter than the five-year average. Soybeans were rated 48 percent good or excellent.</p>
<p>Spring wheat was 51 percent jointed, 19 percentage points behind the five year average. It was rated 54 percent good or excellent.</p>
<p>((Chicago Newsdesk 312-408-8720))</p>
